 def:"Demand is the quantity of a good that buyers are willing and able to buy at each price; supply is the quantity that sellers are willing and able to offer at each price, other things being equal.",
 text:["Demand and supply describe two sets of plans. The demand curve shows how much buyers would purchase at each price, holding everything else constant, and the law of demand says that quantity demanded tends to fall as the price rises. The supply curve shows how much producers would offer, and it usually slopes upwards because a higher price makes extra output worth producing.",
  "The most useful distinction in the topic is between a movement along a curve and a shift of the whole curve. A change in the good's own price moves buyers and sellers along their curves. A change in anything else shifts a curve: income, tastes and the prices of substitutes and complements on the demand side; costs, technology, taxes, subsidies, expectations and the number of firms on the supply side.",
  "Getting this right is what makes later analysis possible. A drought, a fall in input costs or a rise in household incomes can each be traced to the curve it moves, and from there to its likely effect on price and quantity."],
