 def:"Market equilibrium is the price and quantity at which the quantity demanded equals the quantity supplied, so that there is no pressure on the price to change.",
 text:["In a competitive market nobody sets the price. If the price is above equilibrium, sellers cannot sell all they offer and the surplus pushes the price down; below it, buyers cannot find enough and the shortage bids the price up. The equilibrium is where these pressures settle.",
  "Prices do three jobs on the way. They signal where resources are wanted, they give producers and consumers an incentive to respond, and they ration what is available among buyers. At equilibrium, consumer surplus and producer surplus together are as large as they can be, which is why economists treat the competitive outcome as a benchmark for efficiency, though not necessarily for fairness.",
  "Governments sometimes stop the price from adjusting. A maximum price set below equilibrium creates a persistent shortage and rationing by other means; a minimum price set above it creates a surplus that someone, often the government, has to buy or store. Judging such controls means weighing who gains against the losses they create."],
 measure:"The size of a shortage or surplus is the gap between quantity demanded and quantity supplied at a given price. Consumer and producer surplus are the areas between the curves and the price, read from a diagram.",
 terms:[["Excess demand","Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at the current price: a shortage."],
  ["Excess supply","Quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded: a surplus."],
  ["Price mechanism","Prices signalling, giving incentives and rationing."],
  ["Consumer surplus","What buyers are willing to pay above what they actually pay."],
  ["Producer surplus","What sellers receive above the lowest price they would accept."],
  ["Price ceiling and price floor","A legal maximum below equilibrium, and a legal minimum above it."]],
